===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|NL.|Prūssia}}<!--not 'Borussia'-->, a Latinization used by Peter of Dusburg of a {{der|en|bat}} ({{der|en|prg|-}}, or perhaps {{der|en|lt|-}} or {{der|en|lv|-}}) autonym.  The {{der|en|ine-pro|-}} source of the name is unclear; more at [[w:Old Prussians#Etymology|Prussia]]. Compare the {{etyl|ine-bsl-pro|lt}} {{m|ine-bsl-pro|*prus-sk-}}<ref name="d2008">{{R:Derksen 2008|423}}</ref>, whose cognates include {{cog|sa|प्रुष्णोति|tr=pruṣṇóti|gloss=sprinkle}}<ref name="d2008"></ref>, {{cog|cs|prskat|gloss=splutter, sizzle}}<ref name="d2008"></ref> and {{cog|sh|prskati|gloss=splash}}<ref name="d2008"></ref>, and thus signifying "watery land".

The {{cog|enm|-}} designation for the region, {{m|enm|Pruce}}, derives from the same Latinization and is the source of the terms {{m|en|pruce}} and {{m|en|spruce}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|/ˈpɹʌʃə/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ʌʃə|lang=en}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|historical}} A geographical area on the Baltic coast of northeastern Europe.
# {{lb|en|historical}} A Baltic country located in this area, conquered by the [[w:Teutonic Order|Teutonic Order]] and ultimately absorbed into Germany.
# {{lb|en|historical}} A German province which was originally located in this area but later greatly expanded, and which was the predecessor to and a member of the [[w:German Empire|German Empire]]; abolished as an administrative unit at the end of the Second World War.

====Usage notes====
In the Baltic languages the 'u' is long; it was also long in [[W:Middle English|Middle English]], but it has become short in modern English.

